# B.1
sub1("A diagnostic accuracy study asks how well the tool detects impairment and requires roughly 240 participants to answer with any precision (Appendix I); a feasibility study asks whether elderly Libyan participants can complete the battery of four core assessment tasks plus one optional exploratory Qur'anic recitation probe, whether the recording setup produces analysable audio in a real clinic, whether the Arabic feature extractors run correctly on genuine Libyan dialectal speech rather than the Modern Standard Arabic they were drafted against, what the measurements look like in cognitively healthy Libyan speakers, and whether the closed-class word lists — fillers, vague nouns, demonstratives — are appropriate for Libyan dialect. **No diagnostic accuracy figure will be computed from this pilot and no Arabic AUC will be reported from it.** The need is documented in Chapter 1 and section 6.4 — no population-level dementia prevalence study exists for Libya [1, 2, 3], and no publicly available Arabic corpus pairs impaired and healthy speakers on a connected-speech task — and the instrument's Arabic component is an implemented method with no clinical validation; this pilot is the first step toward closing that gap.",
     "A diagnostic accuracy study asks how well the tool detects impairment and requires roughly 240 participants to answer with any precision (Appendix I); a feasibility study asks whether elderly Libyan participants can complete the four core tasks plus the optional exploratory Qur'anic recitation probe, whether the recording setup produces analysable audio in a real clinic, whether the Arabic feature extractors run correctly on Libyan dialectal speech rather than the Modern Standard Arabic they were drafted against, what the measurements look like in cognitively healthy Libyan speakers, and whether the closed-class word lists — fillers, vague nouns, demonstratives — fit Libyan dialect. **No diagnostic accuracy figure will be computed from this pilot and no Arabic AUC will be reported from it.** The need is documented in Chapter 1 and section 6.4 — no population-level dementia prevalence study exists for Libya [1, 2, 3], and no publicly available Arabic corpus pairs impaired and healthy speakers on a connected-speech task — and the instrument's Arabic component is an implemented method with no clinical validation.")
# B.3
sub1("The remedy for the instrument's weakest figure — its specificity on the locked external corpus at the pre-specified threshold, diagnosed in section 5.13 as a threshold-referencing failure — is control-referenced thresholding: the operating threshold set at a fixed percentile of the local cognitively healthy score distribution, so that specificity is fixed by construction and does not move with local prevalence. **That rule needs healthy speakers and no patients, which is precisely what this study's healthy stratum is**, so one recruitment delivers both the Arabic feature validation and the normative distribution from which a Libyan operating threshold can be derived.",
     "The remedy for the instrument's weakest figure — its specificity on the locked external corpus at the pre-specified threshold, diagnosed in section 5.13 as a threshold-referencing failure — is control-referenced thresholding: the operating threshold set at a fixed percentile of the local cognitively healthy score distribution, so that specificity is fixed by construction and does not move with local prevalence. **That rule needs healthy speakers and no patients, which is what this study's healthy stratum is**, so one recruitment delivers both the Arabic feature validation and the normative distribution from which a Libyan operating threshold can be derived.")
sub1("At 20 healthy participants the derived threshold is *provisional* — prospective specificity anywhere between 56% and 91% — and still a decisive improvement on a threshold transplanted from American cohorts, whose specificity in a Libyan clinic is unestimable. The 20-participant minimum stands for feasibility, every healthy participant beyond it directly buys threshold precision, and n ≥ 30 is the point at which the normative deliverable becomes worth publishing; roughly 59 buy a ±10-point interval and 108 a ±7.5-point one, figures for a follow-on normative study. The interval is a floor on the uncertainty: the Beta law assumes the control sample is drawn from the population the threshold will serve, and a convenience sample of families of clinic attendees and staff relatives is plausibly healthier, better educated and younger than the population a deployed instrument would meet. A healthier-than-typical control sample produces lower scores, so the derived threshold sits lower than it should and the instrument over-refers rather than under-refers; that error direction favours sensitivity over missed cases, but excess false positives remain an equity, anxiety and resource-use concern, so education, residence and age are recorded for every healthy participant and the threshold is reported recomputed with the sample reweighted to the local population where census figures allow. The in-sample bootstrap interval reported for the rule on the external corpus answers how precisely the rule hits its own target; the Beta interval answers what a new population would experience; the two are never interchanged.",
     "At 20 healthy participants the derived threshold is *provisional* — prospective specificity anywhere between 56% and 91% — and still a decisive improvement on a threshold transplanted from American cohorts, whose specificity in a Libyan clinic is unestimable. The 20-participant minimum stands for feasibility, every healthy participant beyond it buys threshold precision, and n ≥ 30 is the point at which the normative deliverable becomes worth publishing; roughly 59 buy a ±10-point interval and 108 a ±7.5-point one, figures for a follow-on normative study. The interval is a floor on the uncertainty: the Beta law assumes the control sample is drawn from the population the threshold will serve, and a convenience sample of families of clinic attendees and staff relatives is plausibly healthier, better educated and younger than the population a deployed instrument would meet. A healthier-than-typical control sample produces lower scores, so the derived threshold sits too low and the instrument over-refers rather than under-refers — an error direction that favours sensitivity over missed cases, though excess false positives remain an equity, anxiety and resource-use concern — so education, residence and age are recorded for every healthy participant and the threshold is also reported recomputed with the sample reweighted to the local population where census figures allow. The in-sample bootstrap interval reported for the rule on the external corpus answers how precisely the rule hits its own target; the Beta interval answers what a new population would experience; the two are never interchanged.")
