sub1("This study cannot test the referential deficit index, and the arithmetic belongs in the protocol rather than being discovered later. The index's pronoun-free form, which is structurally what the Arabic index is, reaches AUC 0.596 on Pittsburgh and 0.625 on the age- and sex-matched subset (section 5.10.1), so the expectation for an Arabic dementia sample is roughly 0.60; detecting a true AUC of 0.60 at 80% power requires 130 participants per group, and this study's 20 per group detects only a true AUC of 0.748 or larger (Hanley–McNeil, two-sided α = 0.05; Table (B.6)), so a null result here would be uninterpretable and must not be reported as evidence against the index. No effect this project has measured is that large, and a study that reported a null at this size would be reporting the sample size, not the instrument; the sizes that could test either effect are about 130 per group for the mild-impairment discourse effect and 141 for the dementia referential effect (Appendix I). Two earlier figures in this section were corrected and the corrections are kept on the record: 75 per group for the discourse effect rested on a selection-inflated estimate withdrawn in section 5.16, and a detectable AUC of 0.645 was the figure for sixty per group, not twenty.",
     "This study cannot test the referential deficit index, and the arithmetic belongs in the protocol rather than being discovered later. The index's pronoun-free form, structurally what the Arabic index is, reaches AUC 0.596 on Pittsburgh and 0.625 on the age- and sex-matched subset (section 5.10.1), so the expectation for an Arabic dementia sample is roughly 0.60; detecting a true AUC of 0.60 at 80% power requires 130 participants per group, and this study's 20 per group detects only a true AUC of 0.748 or larger (Hanley–McNeil, two-sided α = 0.05; Table (B.5)), so a null result here would be uninterpretable and must not be reported as evidence against the index. No effect this project has measured is that large; the sizes that could test either effect are about 130 per group for the mild-impairment discourse effect and 141 for the dementia referential effect (Appendix I). Two earlier figures in this section were corrected and the corrections are kept on the record: 75 per group for the discourse effect rested on a selection-inflated estimate withdrawn in section 5.16, and a detectable AUC of 0.645 was the figure for sixty per group, not twenty.")
